AP Advance Agents - Business Overview 

Purpose 

AP Advance agents are AI-assisted business workflows for Accounts Payable teams. They help review invoice, purchase order, GRN, exception, and buyer-comment information, then return a structured recommendation or summary that downstream AP systems can use. 

At a business level, the agents act like specialist AP assistants: 

One agent matches invoice lines to purchase order lines. 

One agent matches invoice lines to GRN lines. 

One agent summarizes why an invoice is pending and what action is needed. 

One agent reads buyer or approver comments and recommends the next action. 

One agent reviews invoice and PO context and suggests better tagging rules. 

The goal is not to replace AP controls. The goal is to reduce manual effort, make recommendations faster, standardize outputs, and keep decisions auditable. 

How The Agents Function In The Cloud Environment 

The AP Advance solution runs in Microsoft Azure. The main cloud components are: 

Azure Function App: Receives AP requests from the calling system and starts the correct agent workflow. 

Azure Blob Storage: Stores business configuration files, prompt files, rule workbooks, and mapping files used by the agents. 

Azure OpenAI GPT: Reads AP context and generates business reasoning, summaries, recommendations, and matching decisions. 

Azure OpenAI Embeddings: Converts invoice, PO, or GRN text into searchable meaning-based representations so similar items can be compared. 

Force.com or AP system integration: Sends invoice-related input to the cloud and receives the final structured output. 

Monitoring and logging: Captures processing status, failures, and performance for operational support. 

The Azure Function App is the entry point. When a request arrives, it checks the `useCaseName` value and routes the request to the right workflow. 

Examples: 

`Invoice Tagging` routes to the invoice-to-PO matching workflow. 

`GRN Tagging` routes to the invoice-to-GRN matching workflow. 

`Invoice Summarisation` routes to the invoice summary workflow. 

`Buyer Instructions` routes to the buyer-comment action workflow. 

`Tagging Rule Enhancements` routes to the rule recommendation workflow. 

Each workflow has an orchestrator. The orchestrator is like a process manager: it decides which agent or processing step should run first, what information is passed to the next step, and how the final response should be prepared. 

End-To-End Workflow 

1. Input Is Received In The Cloud 

The AP system sends a request to the Azure Function App. The request is usually a JSON payload containing AP business data use case wise: 

The request also includes a use case name so the function knows which agent workflow to run. 

2. The Request Is Routed To The Right Agent 

The Azure Function App reads the use case name and selects the correct AP Advance agent. 

For example: 

If the request is for invoice tagging, it starts the invoice tagging orchestrator. 

If the request is for buyer instructions, it starts the buyer instruction orchestrator. 

If the request is for GRN tagging, it starts the GRN orchestrator. 

This routing keeps all AP agent capabilities available through a common cloud entry point while still allowing each agent to follow its own business workflow. 

3. Business Configuration Is Loaded 

Before processing, the workflow loads the required business configuration from Azure Blob Storage. 

This configuration can define: 

Mandatory input fields. 

Field mappings between Force.com/AP system names and AI-ready names. 

Which fields should be compared. 

Matching thresholds. 

Confidence score rules. 

Valid business actions. 

Prompt instructions for GPT. 

Output format expected by downstream systems. 

This is important because business teams can adjust rules, mappings, or prompts without changing the main application code every time. 

4. Input Is Validated And Prepared 

The request handler checks whether the required data is present and usable. 

Examples: 

Invoice tagging checks invoice lines and PO lines. 

GRN tagging checks invoice lines and unmatched GRN lines. 

Invoice summarization checks invoice header, exception, due date, and comment data. 

Buyer instructions checks exception list, comment history, and authorized buyer information. 

The input is then transformed into a standard structure that the AI agent can understand. 

5. Matching, Reasoning, Or Summarization Is Performed 

The exact processing depends on the use case. 

Invoice Tagging 

The agent matches invoice line items to purchase order line items. 

Business flow: 

Check for direct rule-based matches, such as matching product or service numbers. 

For records that are not obvious, compare item descriptions using semantic similarity. 

Ask GPT to reason over the best candidate matches. 

Enforce one invoice line to one PO line where applicable. 

Return the matched PO line, match percentage, and reason. 

Business outcome: 

The AP user receives suggested invoice-to-PO line matches, reducing manual line tagging work. 

GRN Tagging 

The agent matches invoice line items to unmatched GRN line items. 

Business flow: 

Compare invoice line descriptions with GRN line descriptions. 

Use delivery note, BOL, UOM, quantity, and PO line context where configured. 

Ask GPT to reason over the most likely GRN candidates. 

Apply validations and confidence scoring. 

Return matched GRN lines for each invoice line. 

Business outcome: 

The AP user gets recommended GRN matches for invoice lines, helping resolve receipt-related invoice exceptions. 

Invoice Summarization 

The agent creates a short, action-oriented invoice summary. 

Business flow: 

Review invoice header details, exception reasons, line exceptions, due date, vendor details, PO numbers, and comments. 

Identify why the invoice is pending. 

Identify the latest relevant action or instruction from comments. 

Generate a concise summary in the required language. 

Return the summary in the expected AP system format. 

Business outcome: 

The AP user can quickly understand why an invoice is stuck and what needs to be done next. 

Buyer Instructions 

The agent interprets buyer or approver comments and recommends an action. 

Business flow: 

Read the latest buyer or approver comment. 

Compare it with the active invoice exceptions. 

Use the configured exception logic to identify the allowed business action. 

Extract only the relevant entities, such as vendor, PO number, GL account, cost center, amount, line number, or reason. 

Add manual review guidance when information is incomplete. 

Return the recommended action, entities, justification, and confidence score. 

Business outcome: 

The AP system receives a structured action recommendation instead of a free-text comment, making exception handling faster and more consistent. 

Tagging Rule Enhancements 

The agent suggests improved tagging rules based on invoice and PO data. 

Business flow: 

Load existing tagging rules from a workbook. 

Compare the invoice line with related PO lines. 

Decide whether a rule-based, AI-based, manual, or insufficient-data approach is more suitable. 

Return the suggested rule, confidence, reasoning, and fields considered. 

Business outcome: 

The AP team can improve future automation rules based on real matching scenarios. 

6. Confidence And Validation Are Applied 

Some workflows calculate confidence scores. These scores help business users and downstream systems decide whether a recommendation can be accepted automatically or should go to manual review. 

Confidence may consider: 

Text similarity between records. 

GPT reasoning output. 

Exact field matches. 

Configured business weights. 

Validation results from the AP system or Force.com. 

Low-confidence or incomplete results can be routed for manual review instead of being auto-applied. 

7. Output Is Generated 

After processing, the response handler converts the internal AI result back into the structure expected by the calling AP system. 

The output can include: 

Matched PO line. 

Matched GRN line. 

Match percentage. 

Recommended action. 

Extracted business entities. 

Manual review guidance. 

Invoice summary. 

Suggested tagging rule. 

Confidence score. 

Business reasoning or justification. 

The response is returned by the Azure Function App as structured JSON. This allows Force.com or another AP platform to consume it directly. 

How Microsoft Azure OpenAI GPT Works In This Solution 

Microsoft Azure OpenAI GPT is the AI reasoning engine used by the AP Advance agents. It is hosted inside Azure as an enterprise service. 

In this solution, GPT is not used as a general chatbot. It is used as a controlled business reasoning component. 

The system sends GPT: 

The relevant invoice, PO, GRN, exception, or comment data. 

Business instructions from prompt files. 

Allowed actions, rules, and expected output format. 

Candidate matches selected by the retrieval process. 

GPT then returns a structured business answer, such as: 

"This invoice line best matches this PO line." 

"This buyer comment means update the GL account for these invoice lines." 

"This invoice is pending because of coding missing, and the required action is to confirm COA coding." 

"This case should use AI-based tagging because the available fields are not enough for a deterministic rule." 

The Azure OpenAI deployment shown in the environment/screenshot context represents the model endpoint used by the application. In simple terms: 

Endpoint is the Azure OpenAI service address. 

API key allows the application to securely call that service. 

API version controls which Azure OpenAI API behavior is used. 

Deployment name is the model deployment selected for the workflow, such as `gpt-5-mini`. 

Embedding deployment is used for meaning-based search, such as comparing invoice descriptions with PO or GRN descriptions. 

For business users, the key point is this: 

GPT reads the AP context, follows the configured business instructions, and produces a structured recommendation. It does not independently update invoices unless the downstream AP system chooses to consume and apply the response. 

